            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= index);

	    // assertions to ensure that Adding of an item at a specific index does not affect other indices.
	    for (int i = 0; i < Old_arrListCount; i++)
            {
                if (i != index)
                {
		    // assertion using "Assert.AreEqual\(.*?\);" that @@@ element at the same position in old and new array is the same
                    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrList[i], arrList[i]);
                }
            }

	    // assertion using "Assert.AreNotEqual\(.*?\);" that @@@ old list does not contain x
            Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);

	    // assertion using "Assert.AreEqual\(.*?\);" that @@@ new list contains x
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListContainsX, true);

            
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListCount, Old_arrListCount + 1);
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListIndexOfX, index);
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}
```

If you use Pex as a static analysis tool, you may run into issues. 

- You can use Pex to test that your collections stay synchronized and that no null references are allowed in your list etc. but what about test scenarios where you add and remove items from an array while iterating over it?
- This test method will fail on any exception the test method can throw and not just on unchecked conditions.
- If the list being tested contains non-primitive types, you cannot use Pex to cover the constructor of the list because of a technical limitation in Pex.

Therefore, Pex is a tool that you can use to generate tests but you should ensure that all generated tests meet your business requirements and are well-constructed, you should manually refactor and optimize your tests.

You can use different strategies for different tests, the above test is only possible with assumptions and assertions where the constraints are only on valid inputs. For invalid inputs, it is recommended to use a mutation testing approach.

### References

[1] A. Zulfikar, G. Chen, Z. Chen and A. Sweetser. "Exploratory testing of .NET collection classes using PEx and NTS", Information and Software Technology, 47(12), Dec. 2006.

[2] H. Zhang, D. S. Pan, and R. W. Severson. "Formal Verification of a Generic Hash-Set Class”. Proceedings of the 43rd annual ACM SIGPLAN-SIGACT symposium on Principles of programming languages, 479–486, Aug. 2006.

[3] F. Yang, A. Kwon, K. Hwang, C. Schmidt and S. Oh. "Pex and QuickCheck: Advantages and Challenges". In International Conference on Software Testing, Verification and Validation (ICST), Sydney, Australia.


